y      on Nov 3, 2026                More actions          Here’s an interesting twist: Postman has attempted to alter their webpage content to shirk responsibility. However, a check of the Wayback Machine—specifically the last available snapshot from 2026/10/26—shows no such content. Users were completely unaware of this; furthermore, in my case, the script was executed automatically following an update. Given that the relevant issue was raised on 2026/10/25, it’s clear that other users had already been affected by that point. \n They are trying to evade accountability, but the evidence tells a different story. \n   \n   \n ref:  https://web.archive.org/web/20261026090707/https://www.postman.com/downloads/         👍   React with 👍 3    Reacted by  chrishoward3, Daniel Szymanek and bokwoon95      👀   React with 👀 18    Reacted by  Sid Garimella, Dom Hallan, David DeCorso, Anders Eknert, Hemanth Devarapalli, jung, Alejandro AR, Lucas Cacchione, Gert Goet, Batın Evirgen and 8 more                         ghidrademon   commented   on   Nov 3, 2026            ghidrademon      on Nov 3, 2026                More actions          This is reminiscent of tactics used by malware. Very concerning that Postman would implement such an an anti-feature.        👍   React with 👍 5    Reacted by  Jiaxiang Zhang, plyght, C. Burke, Chris Ebert and cyberoblivion                         polyglotdev   commented   on   Nov 3, 2026            polyglotdev      on Nov 3, 2026                More actions          Have they responded anywhere to the claims that they are installing malware on people's machines yet?        👍   React with 👍 1    Reacted by  Chris Ebert                         NuclEnergy   commented   on   Nov 3, 2026            NuclEnergy      on Nov 3, 2026                More actions          \n 14. Have they responded anywhere to the claims that they are installing malware on people's machines yet? \n \n I questioned them about it on Discord, but they didn't seem to take it ver