  "text": "        Postman silently auto-installs AI plugins and attempts to share all API data | Hacker News                  Hacker News   new  |  past  |  comments  |  ask  |  show  |  jobs  |  submit      login                           Postman silently auto-installs AI plugins and attempts to share all API data   (  github.com/postmanlabs  )          1109 points  by  sidgarimella    10 hours ago      |  hide  |  past  |  favorite  |  362 comments                          help    \n      \n                    pants2    10 hours ago       |  next   [–]       \n  It's a shame what happened to Postman. It started as an excellent little developer tool for testing APIs. Then for some reason they raised over $400M in funding and hired 1,500+ employees, for what should have been a tiny OSS/passion project like curl or little snitch. Instead they now have to justify their valuation by forcing logins, collecting data, monthly subscriptions, AI features, etc. I still don't know how they're valued at $5B+ when I don't know a single developer who actually uses it anymore      reply                             swatcoder    10 hours ago       |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  > for some reason they raised over $400M in funding and hired 1,500+ employees You know the reason. A bunch of people were able to take home a lot of money because of that growth and now have the option to FIRE themselves whenever they want to shake off the stress. That's the founder mentality that Y Combinator exists to leverage or encourage and it's ubiquity is what separates the prevailing 21st century technology movement from the nerdy, academic, UNIX-y movement that gave us GNU, linux, curl, ffmpeg, redis, etc We got to watch innovative products get pumped full of helium until they explode with bloat and rent-seeking instead of accumulating a growing foundation of sturdy technical building blocks as we could have had on the other path. AI now leads us into some new movement, the worst version of which a few model vendors protected by regulations absorb almost all technical accomplishment and lease it back out to everybody else, and the best version of which democratizes technical construction so widely that individuals can naively spin up effective solutions for the most idiosyncratic needs. We'll see how it goes.      reply                             mifydev    9 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  I miss the UNIX-y movement so much :(      reply                             antod    6 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  Yeah. The plucky rebels pretty much won against the evil empire, but the forces of darkness weren't completely beaten. They regrouped and then repurposed the rebels tools against them. The might of venture capital, big tech, SaaS, hyperscalers and AI was too much. The rebels weren't decisively beaten so much as eventually eroded out of any real significance.      reply                             Vaushite    3 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  It helps that the rebels donated copies of all their equipment to the dark forces.      reply                             satvikpendem    2 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  MIT and other non copy left licenses were a mistake. In the 2000s and 2010s people were so concerned with usage and GitHub stars of their OSS that they just gave everything away to corporations and now don't have the right to complain anymore.      reply                             ThrowawayR2    5 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Like, what?  Solaris/SunOS, DEC Ultrix/Tru64, AIX, HP-UX, Xenix, etc., all with big bad monopolist AT&T's UNIX as their grandpappy.  UNIX was corporate to the hilt.      reply                             jasomill    4 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  Corporate, sure, but painting DEC, (old) HP, and Sun with the same brush as AT&T, IBM, and Microsoft just because they sold proprietary, but generally well-documented and reliable hardware and software at whatever prices the market would bear seems unfair.      reply                             zaik    9 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  It's not dead??      reply                             conductr    8 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  It’s as dead as rock and roll      reply                             rnd0    31 minutes ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  but the heart of GNU is still beating!      reply                             kyleee    1 hour ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Rock vive no muere      reply                             bigyabai    8 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  UNIX is, UNIX-y stuff isn't.      reply                             cnity    9 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  You are good at writing. Thanks for this.      reply                             oytmeal    5 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  Agree... I scrolled back up from the last of the comments just to say this.      reply                             godelmachine    8 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Yes, this dude is really good at writing.      reply                             krazydad    2 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Very well written reply that voices something I knew but couldn’t quite articulate.      reply                             agorabinary    8 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Articulate and well spoken - I hope that through threads like these that especially tech newcomers discover that there are alternatives to the YC founder mentality of \"growth at all costs\". A more gradual approach that respects users is also better for founders. You may not retire at 35 with some big exit or raise, but why is that the goal anyway? Build something that respects your users and you can see yourself working on for 30 years, living comfortably and in moderation. A minor tweak to The Parable of the Mexican Fisherman[1]: The venture capitalist laughed and said, “That’s the best part. When the time was right, you would announce an IPO, and sell your company stock to the public and become very rich. You would make millions!” “Millions – then what?” [1]  https://aliabdaal.com/newsletter/the-parable-of-the-mexican-...       reply                             johnnyanmac    6 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  >\". A more gradual approach that respects users is also better for founders. You may not retire at 35 with some big exit or raise, but why is that the goal anyway? Well, living on the other side of the pond: I get shuffled around and laid off every few years in the game industry and any savings I had got obliterated surviving in this not-recession as everyone around me gaslights me into telling me that \"the economy is fine\", while doing regular layoffs in nearly all sectors. And it's not like I have some sort of high respect for my craft by those who enjoy it either. There's definitely a temptation to just sell out, find a huge buyout, and get out of the rat race so I can tinker to my heart's content on stuff I want to make. Not what \"sells\" or what some director wanted after 20 filters of HR, \"focus groups, and other sales people get their hands on it. What integrity am I truly keeping in the former situation? Of course, all this assumes I have the opportunity to sell out. Someone at that point is more than likely someone who was already in a trajectory to defy the typical 9-5 40 hour grind to begin with.      reply                             terribleperson    9 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  I'm already enjoying the second version. I just hope we get to keep it.      reply                             guitarlimeo    9 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Well put, hopefully it's the latter version.      reply                             cluckindan    9 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Enshittification and private equity are synonymous. Strange that people are still surprised by this.      reply                             abirch    9 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  Perpetual growth at all costs. No idea what's so bad about a mature stable product/company      reply                             kulahan    5 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  It’s basically wondering why your sales aren’t going up when there are still customers to be found. Even if you saturate a market, new people are born every day, at a greater rate than they die, so one would assume you grow or fade, unless customers just like… stop buying things in a category for some reason (maybe a superior category was invented?) The problem isn’t infinite growth in a still-growing world, it’s exactly what you posted here: Growth at  all  costs. The pendulum has swung too hard in favor of growth over stability. This is great for shareholders (read:everyone in America who wants to realistically retire one day) but bad for workers (read: everyone in America, regardless of whether or not they want to retire one day).      reply                             cluckindan    4 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  Growth will stop when nobody can buy.      reply                             bell-cot    7 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Late-stage capitalism is addicted to ever-increasing quantities of money. It'd be nice to imagine our society surviving our ruling classes' addiction, but I would not bet on that. (You might read some essays by recovered addicts to get a sense of the monomania of it, and why they wouldn't about things like \"mature\" and \"stable\".)      reply                             m0llusk    1 hour ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  Capitalism is a system that needs to be controlled. Setting the kitchen on fire is not late stage food preparation. Crashing a car because you drove fast on bald tires is not late stage vehicular mobility.      reply                             rafram    8 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  I don't think Postman has any PE connections.      reply                             drBonkers    8 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  Wikipedia states Venture capital (VC) is a form of private equity.  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Venture_capital       reply                             Vaushite    3 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Enshittification and capitalism are synonymous. There's no other magical form of equity that's any better. Steam's an oft cited counterexample but Steam is in enshittification stage 2 (once you have the users, be good to vendors to develop total platform lock-in).      reply                             vrganj    9 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n   Hoarders can get piles of money, That is true, hackers, that is true. But they cannot help their neighbors; That's not good, hackers, that's not good. Join us now and share the software; You'll be free, hackers, you'll be free.  -- The Free Software Song, as written and sung by rms  https://www.gnu.org/music/free-software-song.en.html       reply                             netik    8 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  While I agree with every word of this I never want to hear RMS sing this again.      reply                             cowlevel    2 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  You won't be free though, you'll be living under a bridge if you don't get paid. The original free software movers had tenure.      reply                             mschuster91    9 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  > AI now leads us into some new movement, the worst version of which a few model vendors protected by regulations absorb almost all technical accomplishment and lease it back out to everybody else, and the best version of which democratizes technical construction so widely that individuals can naively spin up effective solutions for the most idiosyncratic needs. Yeah and the issues from these vibecoded one-off programs will be felt for generations. I got out of IT partially because I could see the writing on the wall. The \"load bearing Excel sheet\" is one thing, manageable if you could catch the person who wrote it in time before they depart or die and know your way around Excel - there's only so many ways to achieve a desired goal (unless the author used VBA, at which point all bets are off, and I member encountering a spreadsheet that macro-called in a DLL shipped together with the sheet). But vibe coded AI bullshit? That is supposed to be hosted by someone and kept secure (i.e. isn't hackable in a few seconds by anyone who manages to compromise some dingus's laptop? Oh hell no. I'm outta there, I'm too old for this crap.      reply                             robotnikman    9 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  >and I member encountering a spreadsheet that macro-called in a DLL shipped together with the sheet That sounds absolutely horrifying. I never encountered one of those before thankfully.      reply                             akiselev    8 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  Oh it gets better (not the OP but I think the following is even worse). That DLL hooks into SCADA and sometimes sends updates to a PLC controlling safety critical equipment. When does it send updates versus just query the controller? No one knows. The guy who wrote it died in an (unrelated) industrial accident involving a runaway forklift after leaving the company. Even so much as debugging it would require shutting down the entire factory and replacing all the crucibles afterwards ($$$).      reply                             jasomill    4 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  That's even worse than the time I was on a conference call with the COO of a Fortune 500 retailer who referred to \"the Excel spreadsheet we use to keep track of the stores we own\". The subject matter was building an auditing system to comply with the requirements of an IRS consent decree as part of a settlement for shoddy accounting practices which may or may not have included tracking billions of dollars of assets with Excel.      reply                             jodrellblank    8 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  > When does it send updates versus just query the controller? No one knows. Ask an LLM to reverse engineer it and explain it.      reply                             jacobgkau    8 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  They said this: > Even so much as debugging it would require shutting down the entire factory and replacing all the crucibles afterwards ($$$). I'm guessing the fundamental reason debugging would require that isn't something an AI could get around. My guess is the LLM would immediately disregard whatever instructions you gave it not to cause having to replace all the crucibles, then say \"Oops! You're right.\" when you point out that you asked it not to do that.      reply                             wholinator2    5 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  Hmmm, but shouldn't there be some way to just make a copy of the file, put in a container and tell the AI, scan the environment, do nothing except watch what the excel sheet does and record it, then determine how to create an identical sandbox containing the same outputs and feedback. Then see if the sheet works in that sandbox. If it does, then debug it until you get identical behavior out of a new program. Then have a week/month of dry runs just copying the inputs from the real world to it and capturing its output to compare to the dll. Do that for long enough and you should have a drag and drop solution right? I'm not saying it's easy, or worth anyone's time, but it should be possible right?      reply                             Ekaros    8 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Thankfully we are finally after all these years getting regulation on that sort of stuff. It seems EU was 20 something years late... And no I really think that regulation in case like this is really reasonable idea.      reply                             fock    9 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  sadly this is coming to your linux-distro in no time! Far too many single-dev core building block which are now adding features at breakneck speed conversing with AI only.      reply                             pessimizer    9 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  > But vibe coded AI bullshit? That is supposed to be hosted by someone and kept secure It's also astonishingly stupid. I don't want a one-off program for everything. I want a well-designed and battle-tested program that I can  extend  with AI, and that has a ton of people's AIs directed at it, constantly finding bugs, posting them on the bugtracker, and writing and debating pull requests. I can't comprehend people who think that everything should be built over and over again, and that that will be safe, practical or cheap. It's like they're telling me that I should design a new language for each conversation that I have.  Why would I?  The worst part is how far the capabilities of AI are from that dream anyway. They can't even write readable, intelligible code. They're being celebrated for being able to notice buffer overrun errors in code that people would also notice if anybody had ever really looked at some particular span of code in the last 10 years. It's finding errors in move fast and break things code and code that has been maintained by a weirdo professor in his spare time for free and half the economy somehow relies on. That's not magic, its a sign of how sloppy things got.      reply                             phil21    6 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  Because IT/programmers/tech folks in general have abdicated their responsibilities for too long, imo. The industry collectively started huffing their own farts and forgot they operate in a support role for most organizations. The state of IT has been stagnant at best for the past 20-30 odd years. The promises of the 90's or earlier that we were going to have average office workers being able to automate their workflows never was realized. Today with AI I'm watching random sales people automate a large portion of the bullshit part of their jobs IT and technology systems foisted upon them for no real reason. Those CRM systems that force you to click 28 buttons per lead you enter into the system, with all sorts of amazing UIx like having to restart from scratch if you miss a field, get logged out during data entry, etc. This is the standard state of most software people interact with on a daily basis. If current IT didn't  absolutely suck  for most people, and hadn't become a means to it's own ends I think you'd see a lot less pent up demand for these vibe coded one-off applications that solve a specific problem for a single user. It's been interesting to watch. Normies can finally get stuff done without the IT and developer gatekeeping and get on with doing the meaningful bits of their jobs. For better or worse. It's certainly going to blow up in quite a few cases, but I think the cat is out of the bag if progress in tooling - not even models - continues at a rapid pace.      reply                             jasomill    4 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  This sounds exactly like the promises of putting a PC on everyone's desktop rather than a terminal connected to a locked-down mainframe, which in the end led to the lockdown migrating to PCs and the few employees who had any interest in automation forced to use tools like Excel because it was at least an improvement over VBScript and batch files and IT wouldn't let them install legit development tools unless their job description required them. What are the odds that the average corporate IT department is going to allow the use of anything other than locked-down corporate AI with strict token quotas and stricter acceptable use policies?      reply                             jaapz    6 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  >  They can't even write readable, intelligible code. That really is no longer an up to date view of LLM's. Frontier models can and do write readable, intelligible code. They can also write shit code. Kind of like human devs.      reply                             2muchcoffeeman    7 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Because even with AI I don’t have infinite capacity to make every piece of software well designed and I have other deadlines. > They can't even write readable, intelligible code.  Skill issue.      reply                          CamperBob2    9 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [10 more]       \n [flagged]                              birdsongs    9 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  True or not - this vibe of \"deal with it, it's how things are\" feels really crummy to me. There's legitimate concerns and emotions here. No reason to be a dick when discussing it.      reply                             ericfr11    9 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Poor judgement call      reply                             CamperBob2    8 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  We'll see, won't we.      reply                             surgical_fire    9 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  > Deal with it. Whenever I spot this shitty cop out here, it is always on a bad take. Never fails.      reply                             bigstrat2003    8 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  Yeah, I was going to vouch the comment because while I think it's a bad take, it doesn't really break the rules to have bad takes. Then I got to the arrogant \"deal with it\", and I think that does cross the line.      reply                             CamperBob2    7 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  \"Deal with it\" crosses the line, but \"bullshit\" and \"crap\" doesn't.  Got it.      reply                             johnnyanmac    6 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  One pretty much exclusively shuts down discussion. The others can be use to dismiss discussion, but not exclusively.      reply                             surgical_fire    7 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  >  Got it. It's how things work. Deal with it.      reply                             CamperBob2    4 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  Shooting the messenger does not, in fact, work.      reply                             tonymet    8 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  trying to make money off an invention is perfectly reasonable.      reply                             andyroid    8 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  Indeed, a couple of GUI forms on top of curl is right up there with electricity, antibiotics and sliced bread.      reply                             tonymet    8 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  then why haven't you done it?      reply                             dspillett    4 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  Because we don't have that special sort of brain that thinks turning such a simple useful tool into a commercial behemoth is a good idea? I doubt anyone there really things there is a useful purpose to it all, but they have to keep it in motion to keep their pay coming in.      reply                             tonymet    3 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  the world is what you make of it.  make something, sell it for exactly what you need to buy bread .  I don't see why being so cynical is necessary. Right now your boss has sold something, and pays you with the remainder.  Is he a bad guy? What's wrong if you did the same?      reply                             jodrellblank    8 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Silently installing hooks into your other tools, to route their traffic through Postman without your knowledge or consent, is not at all reasonable.      reply                             tonymet    8 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  you skipped a node in the tree      reply                             kentm    8 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  How you try to make money off an invention may be perfectly reasonable, or it may be perfectly unreasonable.  The \"how\" matters here.      reply                             tonymet    8 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  nearly every \"how\" is going to trigger some haters.      reply                             preg_match    7 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  Yes but they’re clearly not all equal. Not everyone is a “hater” for thinking that basically installing malware to make money is bad. There are legitimate, ethical ways to make money, and then not so ethical ones. It seems for tech companies, they often can’t find the ethical ways or they simply don’t produce enough money, and so they feel forced to act unethically. The only reason they get away with it is… well everyone is doing it. It’s a culture problem, and it’s viral to the entire tech economy. Nobody cares about being scummy, doing things behind users backs, invading their privacy, employing dark patterns, or even leaking highly sensitive data.      reply                             tonymet    5 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  this is a bit of a bait and switch.  The immediate comment was about opportunism in general.      reply                             Ambolia    8 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  People hating you for the damage you cause while making money is also perfectly reasonable.      reply                             tonymet    5 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  People here forgetting how forums work.      reply                             MiroslavPokorny    1 hour ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Postman is not an invention. Its not that complicated, most engineers could write their own tool in a day or two.      reply                             theturtletalks    9 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  It’s definitely the best version. I’m seeing so many amazing open source projects since people who once had the vision, but not the coding chops, can now build what they imagine. I myself am building a personal OS that connects to everything and it’s way more fun to build things and use them yourself vs just paying for some other software. Especially when that software can be enshittified or start to squeeze its users like you mentioned.      reply                             caned    8 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  I'm also seeing people who previously had little interest in coding or understanding systems suddenly become empowered to pump out and deploy unmaintainable trash faster than their peers can resolve the problems they generate.      reply                             theturtletalks    8 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  Suit yourself. Always diamonds in the rough and you can check repos I star on Github. I built software to manage my own e-commerce business before AI coding existed but see teenagers I mentor vibe code applications that are actually helping them make money. They are 5-7 years ahead of where I was at their age and I started the e-commerce business in college. And these people are making software for themselves and opensourcing it. So what are you on about peers resolving problems?      reply                             caned    7 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  One effect doesn't exclude the other. I'm talking about people creating more problems than they are solving because they lack a fundamental understanding of how their systems, and the generated code that acts upon them, actually work. They're enamored with the ease and alacrity with which they can generate code but unwittingly leave a host of issues to be cleaned up. It's usually other people who discover the problems because these suddenly productive \"authors\" didn't have the know-how and awareness to create exacting enough prompts or sufficiently iterate on the generated output. It's a phenomenon I've observed which doesn't necessarily take away from the benefits you've found. But maybe it's worth some consideration that this shift in empowerment isn't without negative consequences.      reply                             theturtletalks    7 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  Have you built something you actually use? The feedback loop is immediate. You tell Claude and it goes it and fixes it. You realize mostly proprietary software is made by people that haven’t even worked in that niche? Now people in that niche with these immediate lo