 "page:https://simonwillison.net/2026/Nov/3/qwen-can-interpret-model-weights/": {
  "v": 3,
  "fetched_at": "2026-11-04T04:19:23.599Z",
  "bytes": 10222,
  "text": " \n \n \n \n \n \n Qwen 3.8 27B on my laptop can interpret model weights, too \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n\n  \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n\n\n\n\n \n \n \n\n \n   \n      Simon Willison’s Weblog  \n     Newsletter \n   \n  \n\n\n \n   \n     Sponsored by:  Greptile &mdash; Your code reviewer uses Computer Use to catch bugs by opening your app and clicking through it. Now you can configure its test environment and define the key user journeys it tests.  Try it for free \n   \n \n\n\n \n \n\n \n\n\n \n Qwen 3.8 27B on my laptop can interpret model weights, too \n 3rd November 2026 \n\n\n\n Yesterday’s hot paper was  Sparks of Weight-Space Programming: LLMs can natively read & write neural network weights  by Rikiya Takehi, Jayden Teoh, Alex Zhang, and Omar Khattab. The paper (OK, the authors classify is as a “blog post”, but it’s paper-shaped) found that frontier models, in particular GPT-6 Astra, can both interpret and modify the raw weights of a machine learning model—literally a text description of the model containing a bunch of floating point numbers. I’ve been playing with one of the examples from the paper and I’m delighted to share that Qwen 3.8 27B running on my laptop can do this as well! \n Weight-Space Programming \n Here’s the highlight from their abstract, describing impressive capabilities for GPT-6 Astra and coining the term  Weight-Space Programming  for when LLMs analyze and modify model weights: \n \n Just by looking at the raw weights, GPT-6 Astra  can tell what a network was trained to do . And without any environment feedback, it  has no trouble writing, from scratch, small game-playing networks  that outplay networks trained with PPO for 100 million steps. \n Likewise, asked to make Qwen3.5-0.8B better at math without gradients, Astra  identifies an issue in its weights  causing reasoning loops that Qwen’s own  model card  warns about, and  fixes this by natively editing 0.016% of the weights . With the same sampling settings, this weight correction raises MATH-500 from 7.9% to 48.1% and generalizes out of the box to instruction following, raising IFEval by 3x. \n All of this is possible without allowing Astra to write training loops. It uses its reasoning to figure out what each part of a neural network computes, or should compute, and changes the weights accordingly. We call this  Weight-Space Programming . \n \n The paper combines two topics that I think deserve to be treated separately. The first is what models can do with raw weights  without  running tools or executing code. The second explores how well models, once armed with code, can run operations to update and improve other models. \n spaCy author Matthew Honnibal  called the paper misleading  for conflating the two, and Sean Goedecke  deconstructed the Qwen3.5-0.8B update example  as taking the same steps that a human ML researcher would use. \n I’m going to focus on the no-tools question, which I think is the more interesting of the two. \n The paper presents the first question as  Can LLMs understand model behaviors from weights?  They trained four neural networks to play simplified 10x10 grid versions of four classic computer games—Breakout, Pong, Pac-Man, and Freeway (I had  to look that one up , it’s an Atari 2600 game from 1981). Then they fed those weights (as floating point numbers) to an LLM along with the following question: \n \n  The weights below belong to one policy network that was trained with reinforcement learning to play a game on this grid. Which game was it trained on? Work from the weights. You have no tools: no code execution, no calculator, nothing to run. This is a single response.  \n \n The GitHub repository that accompanies the paper is  RikiyaT/lm_understand-logs . The  exp1_which_game  folder contains the prompts they used, with weights for the four games: \n \n  breakout/prompt.txt  \n  freeway/prompt.txt  \n  pacman/prompt.txt  \n  pong/prompt.txt  \n \n I checked and, aside from the weights, each prompt is identical to the others. \n Trying it out with Qwen 3.8 27B and other models \n The prompts on GitHub are self-contained. My  LLM  tool has a  -f/--fragment  option which can be passed the URL to a text file to use as a prompt, which means I can run any of the above like this: \n  llm -f https://raw.githubusercontent.com/RikiyaT/lm_understand-logs/refs/heads/main/exp1_which_game/runs/breakout/prompt.txt \\\n  -m openrouter/moonshotai/kimi-k3.1-pro  \n This example requires the  llm-openrouter plugin  installed, and runs the Breakout prompt against Kimi K3.1 Pro via OpenRouter. Here’s  what I got back from Kimi K3.1 Pro , which successfully guessed Breakout after 9m 41s and 19,493 reasoning tokens. \n But how about  Qwen 3.8 27B ? That’s still my favorite model to run on my own laptop—it’s just 17GB (I use  this version ) and somehow feels close to the leading proprietary models of 2025. \n I fired up  LM Studio , ensured Qwen 3.8 27B was loaded with a long context window (the default 8192 context failed with errors), installed the  llm-lmstudio  plugin and ran this command: \n  LMSTUDIO_TIMEOUT=900 \\\n  llm -f https://raw.githubusercontent.com/RikiyaT/lm_understand-logs/refs/heads/main/exp1_which_game/runs/pacman/prompt.txt \\\n  -m lmstudio/qwen/qwen3.8-27b  \n (I had to bump the timeout up using an environment variable, see  this issue .) \n After 46m 12s and 43,506 reasoning tokens, Qwen on my laptop  returned a response  that ended like this (here’s  the reasoning trace ): \n \n Game: A simplified Pac-Man-style gridworld: an agent moves through a fixed 8x8 maze with central and corner wall blocks, moving objects/ghosts, and reward targets/pellets. \n Description: The agent navigates a fixed maze-like 8x8 board, using current agent/moving-object/previous-frame cues to move through corridors, collect reward-target cells, and avoid moving objects or hazards. \n Confidence: 72% \n \n This is impressive. I tried it on two of the other games as well. It  identified Pong  ( reasoning ) in 1h 4m 54s: \n \n  Pong  — specifically a Pong-like paddle/ball game where the agent controls a vertical paddle on the right side and must move up/down to intercept or return a moving ball/reward object. \n Description: \n The agent moves a right-side paddle vertically to hit or catch a moving ball-like reward target moving across the 8×8 field, while a left-side moving object/opponent and wall borders define the play; reward is obtained by successfully intercepting/returning/scoring the ball, and missing or failing to hit it is penalized. \n Confidence: \n  75%  \n \n And  Breakout  ( reasoning ) in 52m 25s: \n \n Game: Breakout, or an Arkanoid-like paddle/ball/brick game. \n Description: The agent is a paddle on the bottom row and moves left/right to keep a ball in play; the ball moves around the upper grid, and bricks/reward targets in the top rows give reward when hit, while missing the ball/hitting the bottom wall ends the episode or gives penalty. \n Confidence: 88% \n \n Qwen failed at the Freeway prompt, which didn’t surprise me considering that game is more obscure than the other three. Here’s  that transcript —Qwen thought it was “A simplified Pac-Man-like grid game: agent moves 4-directionally”. \n The reasoning traces are fun to skim, and almost incomprehensible. Here’s a sample: \n \n What about agent channel0? We see some large channel0 rows near row8 col7/8 and maybe bottom? Let’s list channel0 large: line436 input435 (row8 col7 ch0) -2.107,1.169,0.513...; line441 input440 (row8 col8 ch0) -0.376,0.010,1.564,3.855...; line445 input444 (row8 col8 ch4) 0.142,0.491...; line382? input381 ch0? Wait line382 input381: 381//5=76 (row7 col6), ch1? 381%5=1? 76 * 5=380, ch1. So not. Channel0 large maybe at row8 col7/8 only. Also top row? line44 input43 ch3, line49 ch3. Channel0 large maybe not many. Agent position could be near bottom-right? If the agent is typically at bottom-right? \n \n That said though, I expect a sufficiently experienced human being armed with a notepad and a pencil could get there with enough effort. \n It’s a cool paper, but some of the wider commentary I’ve seen around it does not feel justified. I’m not ready to declare that the age of source code is over and our future involves incomprehensible machines editing their own model weights in incomprehensible ways. \n\n\n \n Posted  3rd November 2026  at 4:29 pm &middot; Follow me on  Mastodon ,  Bluesky ,  Twitter  or  subscribe to my newsletter  \n  \n\n \n\n\n \n More recent articles \n \n  \n      The U.S. Census Bureau and “Super Intelligence”  - 2nd November 2026 \n  \n      The October inflection point for vibe game development  - 28th October 2026 \n  \n      Battle Drones - a vibe coded RTS with audio-based multiplayer  - 20th October 2026 \n  \n      Hello Python 3.15, goodbye 3.10, and now I can use 3.11  - 12th October 2026 \n  \n \n \n\n\n\n   \n\n \n\n \n This is  Qwen 3.8 27B on my laptop can interpret model weights, too  by Simon Willison, posted on  3rd November 2026 . \n\n\n    \n         \n            machine-learning\n             70 \n         \n    \n         \n            ai\n             2,308 \n         \n    \n         \n            generative-ai\n             2,048 \n         \n    \n         \n            local-llms\n             166 \n         \n    \n         \n            llms\n             2,013 \n         \n    \n         \n            qwen\n             64 \n         \n    \n         \n            paper-review\n             20 \n         \n    \n\n\n\n\n  Previous:   The U.S. Census Bureau and “Super Intelligence”  \n\n\n \n   \n    Monthly briefing\n   \n   \n    Sponsor me for  $10/month  and get a curated email digest of the month's most important LLM developments.\n   \n   \n    Pay me to send you less!\n   \n   \n    Sponsor & subscribe\n   \n \n \n\n\n\n   \n   \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n \n     \n        Disclosures  \n        Colophon  \n       &copy; \n        2002  \n        2003  \n        2004  \n        2005  \n        2006  \n        2007  \n        2008  \n        2009  \n        2010  \n        2011  \n        2012  \n        2013  \n        2014  \n        2015  \n        2016  \n        2017  \n        2018  \n        2019  \n        2020  \n        2021  \n        2022  \n        2023  \n        2024  \n        2025  \n        2026  \n       \n         \n           \n           \n           \n           \n           \n           \n         \n       \n     \n \n\n\n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n"
 },
 "page:https://honnibal.dev/blog/astra-read-write-misleading": {
  "v": 3,
  "fetched_at": "2026-11-04T04:19:24.841Z",
  "bytes": 4800,
  "text": "     The 'Astra can read and write weights to improve Qwen' post is misleading · honnibal.dev                                            honnibal.dev       The 'Astra can read and write weights to improve Qwen' post is misleading  2026-11-03  ·  5  minute read    Stage magicians often do the same trick multiple ways.\nOne iteration uses a gimmick prop, the next one shows you the prop isn’t gimmick but that one palms the object, the third time was a forced choice etc.\nWhen you watch the performance you assume there’s only one technique, so each time they do it they seem to debunk your hypothesis about how it\nmust be working. \n This is pretty much how the experiments in the  ‘Astra can read and write weights to improve Qwen’ post  work. The post’s abstract says: \n \n Just by looking at the raw weights, GPT-6 Astra can tell what a network was trained to do. And without any environment feedback, it has no trouble writing, from scratch, small game-playing networks that outplay networks trained with PPO for 100 million steps. \n \n \n Likewise, asked to make Qwen3.5-0.8B better at math without gradients, Astra identifies an issue in its weights causing reasoning loops that Qwen’s own model card warns about, and fixes this by natively editing 0.016% of the weights. With the same sampling settings, this weight correction raises MATH-500 from 7.9% to 48.1% and generalizes out of the box to instruction following, raising IFEval by 3x. \n \n If you read that, would you assume that the Qwen experiment worked ‘just by looking at the raw weights’? Well it doesn’t. There’s experiments that look at raw weights, and experiments that improve a game-playing network, and an experiment that improves Qwen at maths. But those latter two aren’t just working with raw weights, they write Python programs. \n Here’s a summary of what the post actually does and how the techniques work. \n     #  Experiment  Size  Reads raw weights?  Writes weights?  Python?  Runs network?  Result      1  Name the game a PPO MLP was trained on  17K  Yes  No  No  No  4/4 correct    2  Name the shape a diffusion MLP generates  24K  Yes  No  No  No  3/3 correct    3  Name a char transformer’s language and copying heads  7.2M  No  No  Yes  No  3/3 correct    4  Fix a stuck Kinetix car policy  172K  Yes  Yes  No  No¹  0% → 100%²    5  Write MinAtar game MLPs from scratch  20K  No³  Yes  No  No  Beats PPO-100M⁴    6  Write a 9×9 Go network  1.5M  No  Yes  Yes  Yes (0–50 passes)  Beats PPO    7a  Spot the sycophantic Gemma 3  270M  No  No  Yes  No  Failed    7b  Same, seeing attention patterns  270M  No  No  Yes  Yes⁵  3/3 correct    8  Fix Qwen3.5’s maths  800M  No  Yes  Yes  Yes (50 passes)  7.9% → 48.1%    8b  Same, on Gemma 3  270M  No  Yes  Yes  Yes (50 passes)  Failed      ¹ Given one recorded episode of the failure. \n² In one try, changing 3.5% of the weights. \n³ Weights start at zero, so there’s nothing to read. \n⁴ Beats PPO-500M only on Breakout (517 vs. 25); loses to it on Seaquest (53 vs. 89) and Asterix (53 vs. 55). \n⁵ Runs up to 16 texts and sees only the attention patterns, not the outputs.   \n The “just by looking at the raw weights” setting (raw floats in context, no tools, no\nrunning the network) only appears in experiments 1, 2 and 4, on networks of 17K–172K\nweights. Every experiment involving a real language model uses Python, and the\nQwen result, which is the headline one, also gets to run the model on up to 50 batches of practice problems. \n What the Qwen result actually shows is that Astra can be prompted to devise and perform tasks that\na human ML engineer could do. Human ML engineers write snippets of code that analyse or\neven modify network weights “directly” all the time. Figuring out how to do this productively is not\nnothing, and AI models of a year or two ago would have failed at all these tasks. \n But to me the Qwen result and the other experiments that use Python don’t belong in the same post or paper as the experiments that read the weights directly.\nA single technique that could achieve both, “Author weights for a small game from scratch” and “Improve the performance of a large model on a sample task” would imply a deep capability. If you test a single technique that way you’ve demonstrated a sort of ‘gestalt’ of the weights (it can write them out from scratch) and also that it understands a large existing network. But that’s not at all true here, because it’s not a single technique.        Matthew Honnibal  Computational linguist from Sydney based in Berlin, Germany. I'm the author of the  spaCy  Natural Language Processing library and the co-founder of  Explosion , where we're building  Ellf : a platform that helps your team and coding agents build custom, fully private AI solutions in-house.                           honnibal.dev        "
 },
 "page:https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50355251": {
  "v": 3,
  "fetched_at": "2026-11-04T04:19:26.256Z",
  "bytes": 67218,
  "text": "        Qwen 3.8 27B on my laptop can interpret model weights, too | Hacker News                  Hacker News   new  |  past  |  comments  |  ask  |  show  |  jobs  |  submit      login                           Qwen 3.8 27B on my laptop can interpret model weights, too   (  simonwillison.net  )          528 points  by  theanonymousone    10 hours ago      |  hide  |  past  |  favorite  |  166 comments                          help    \n      \n                    variaga    9 hours ago       |  next   [–]       \n  Not making any claims about a specific model's capabilities, but all these \"can interpret model weights\" posts brings to mind the line in Shakespeare's Henry IV  GLENDOWE: I can call spirits from the vasty deep. HOTSPUR: Why, so can I, or so can any man, but will they come when you do call for them?  I can interpret weights too, but will that interpretation be correct?      reply                             layer8    9 hours ago       |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  They did test for correct interpretation, so what point are you making?      reply                             garg    6 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  His point is that he has read Shakespeare but not the linked article.      reply                             3form    4 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  He can call on comments from the vasty deep, but shalt we find them relevant?      reply                             liuliu    6 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  These are famous games tested here though, and I tried to feed one problem to DeepSeek 4.1 Flash 1017 and it comes back at 66% confidence it is a Pac-Man (incorrect). That smells to me the problem setup heavily hint towards these 2D games. Might be resolution related to be honest.      reply                             rich_sasha    1 hour ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  To be honest I think the experiment would be insanely impressive even if you prompted explicitly that the game is one of 4 options.      reply                             nightski    7 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  From what I saw in the paper, no they did not.  They tested that the specific change introduced increased performance on a single task (the one mentioned in the prompt).  They did not study the impact of that change on overall model performance across a variety of benchmarks.  But maybe I missed something.      reply                             lnenad    7 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  But increasing performance on a single task was the point of the change?      reply                             giancarlostoro    9 hours ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  > I can interpret weights too, but will that interpretation be correct? Chances are much higher than a model can figure out what the patterns are, decipher them, and retain that knowledge in a meaningful way it can reason about. Me and you might take years or months to do all of that on our own, with a \"map\" of the weight we might be able to discuss it, but not as effortlessly as an LLM. Honestly, I had told a few different colleagues that reading a model is probably not that exclusive to frontier models. It's probably yet another reason OpenAI /  Anthropic don't release any big open weight models, but thankfully Google and Microsoft release reasonably sized models. Imagine a new GPT open weight release, it would get dissected drastically.      reply                             achiles    2 hours ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  the freeway miss is the tell imo. the one obscure game of the four and it just called it pac-man, which looks like pattern matching toward famous games, not reading what the network computes      reply                             buffalobuffalo    7 hours ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Thinking about it, it makes a lot of sense that LLMs could easily be trained to interpret weights.  The attention mechanism of an LLM is very well suited to analyze a set of weights (in text form), since the attention to each weight in the text via key/query can sort of emulate the connection structure of the network that the weights represent.      reply                             mwernolagoo    9 hours ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Further than that, aren't \"weights\" just another sequence of tokens? We see them as vectors, pass to the API or as strings into a chatbox interface. Is it so surprising that the high-dimensionality linear algebra machine crunches them and gives us back useful vectors that can be mapped to text? Isn't that what this whole technology paradigm is about? How is it surprising? Granted, it is cool. But I see so much of \"look at this cool thing I did with LLMs\" and honestly... we need more structured text, more explicit documentation, more ergonomic and intuitive CRUD applications. The amount of \"look at cool things\" is growing way faster than before and they are all sort of low effort now because the \"agentic\" LLM-VM looping apparatus can do a lot of heavy lifting and run with our prompts. And I mean, if one has jargon, knowledge, and intention, they can also leverage that into great structured text, ergonomic and intuitive CRUD apps etc, all of which add great value to our mundane pursuits of applied research, software products and services and so on. Honestly, I begin to think the problem is people dazzling themselves instead of doing the housekeeping that is the bulk of life. Technology choice is entirely secondary.      reply                          D-Machine    9 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [6 more]       \n [flagged]                              mlyle    8 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  I understand linear algebra.  It seems like he probably does, too. Are you sure you have read and understood his point? It seem quite possible that the flattened-model-weights ingested into a model have a much greater fidelity of representation in a similar model's KV cache than they do in a human's working memory, and that being able to loop and digest and chunk makes it unsurprising that models can excel at this type of task. I think that's the point the person is making.  Do you disagree?  If so, why?      reply                          D-Machine    8 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [5 more]       \n [flagged]                              tomrod    6 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  A vector is a type of a matrix is a type of a tensor.      reply                             mlyle    6 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  > Bringing up the KV cache is a sign you don't understand what you are talking about here. The KV cache is a computational efficiency thing and has no impact on how these models actually work (and isn't needed at all). The vague analogy to human working memory also just takes the cake here. You seem to really like to tell people that they don't know what they're talking about more than you seem to be parsing what they're actually saying. I remember when I used to look for reasons to pounce on people for not knowing what they were talking about based on a superficial thing or two rather than try to understand what they were saying.  I mostly got past it as a teenager, though.      reply                          D-Machine    6 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [3 more]       \n [flagged]                              mlyle    6 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  If you care about the quality of HN: produce the high quality content you want; don't demean others. There's not really a point in talking back to abuse.  Think what you will. I will point out that your comment was the first reply ever to someone completely new to HN, though.      reply                             maxrev17    5 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  I really am not surprised someone is such an arse around this place. Being totally factually right is the most important thing in life always, right?!      reply                             lukan    9 hours ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  And this reminds me of the king in the little prince, who had the power to order the sun to rise or go down. But only once a day at a certain time.      reply                             huijzer    8 hours ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Biblically speaking, spirits from the vasty deep must be demons since they are from below and angels, God and Jesus are from above. So in that sense it’s not very impressive. Demons are something a sane person would rather avoid.      reply                             StableAlkyne    8 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  I mean, if you want to inject religion into this, that interpretation that it \"must be demons\" isn't even supported by the source text. In 1 Samuel, King Saul visits a medium in Endor in order to speak to the (dead) prophet Samuel. Human dead go to Shoel (in familiar terms, it is similar to the Greek underworld) in the Old Testament. Later on in the New Testament, the idea of a  really really bad place  is introduced in the form of Tartarus, which is specifically where the rebelling angels were sent.  But that is not the same place human dead go. The idea that everything \"not Heaven\" must be Hell is an invention, caused mostly by dogma that rose from bad translations of the Greek and Hebrew in the middle ages (for example, King James - itself mostly correcting minor errors of the older Bishop's Bible - refers to all of it as Hell even though the original text most inarguably refers to different places different beings go).      reply                             BalinKing    29 minutes ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  I see your point about Sheol not being Gehenna not being \"the bosom of Abraham\" not being the New Jerusalem and so on, and you might already know everything I'm about to say, but just to clarify for others' sake: I think it's wrong to say that the \"really really bad place\" is uniquely introduced as Tartarus (AFAIK mentioned only once in the NT, in 2 Peter 2:4[0]). Jesus clearly refers to a very unpleasant place in e.g. Mark 9:47–48[1], Luke 16:24,[2] and Matthew 25:41[3] (where it's explicitly stated that false prophets will go to the same place as \"the devil and his angels\"). Other references to an unambiguously bad place, as the sole and final destination of unbelievers, are throughout the New Testament, e.g. the \"lake of fire\" in Revelation 20:15[4] and 21:8[5]. Revelation 20:14[6] even says that \"Hades\" will be thrown into that same \"lake of fire\" (which of course supports what you're saying about Hades being distinct from Hell).\nAs a final note, even the reference to Tartarus in 2 Peter 2:4 speaks of it as a temporary holding place where \"angels who sinned\" are being \"kept for judgment\", implying that there's a more final fate awaiting them (identified in Revelation 20:10[7], at least for Satan, as the aforementioned \"lake of fire\"). The main point I'm trying to make is that, while it's certainly true there are other \"places where dead people/angels go\" than Heaven and Hell, that's only true  at this point in time . To my knowledge, the New Testament is consistent about a final judgment where there are only two possible fates (the New Jerusalem, i.e. Heaven, and the lake of fire, i.e. Hell). [0] \"For if God did not spare angels who sinned, but cast them into the pit [Tartarus] and delivered them to chains of darkness, being kept for judgment;\" [1] \"And if your eye causes you to stumble, gouge it out; it is better for you to enter the kingdom of God with one eye, than, having two eyes, to be cast into hell [Gehenna], where their worm does not die, and the fire is not quenched.\" [2] \"And he cried out and said, ‘Father Abraham, have mercy on me, and send Lazarus so that he may dip the tip of his finger in water and cool off my tongue, for I am in agony in this flame.’\" [3] \"Then He will also say to those on His left, ‘Depart from Me, accursed ones, into the eternal fire which has been prepared for the devil and his angels;\" [4] \"And if anyone’s name was not found written in the book of life, he was thrown into the lake of fire.\" [5] \"But for the cowardly and unbelieving and abominable and murderers and sexually immoral persons and sorcerers and idolaters and all liars, their part will be in the lake that burns with fire and brimstone, which is the second death.\" [6] \"Then death and Hades were thrown into the lake of fire. This is the second death, the lake of fire.\" [7] \"And the devil who deceived them was thrown into the lake of fire and brimstone, where the beast and the false prophet are also, and they will be tormented day and night forever and ever.\"      reply                             snapcaster    8 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Crazy how much the quote went over your head. Literature written for peasants 400 years ago and you can't grasp the point      reply                             coldtea    8 hours ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  > I can interpret weights too, but will that interpretation be correct?  In TFA case, given that they were able to guess what the weights did (which the researchers knew in advance and could cross-check) and to even tweak and improve them, the answer is yes.      reply                             dburkland    6 hours ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Love this reference      reply                             keeganpoppen    7 hours ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  interpretation is