estion remains: isn't something like pi.dev good enough for this? Or are you using custom harness to describe the scripts/skills/etc that you create yourself?      reply                             trog    4 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  I am cheerfully using Opencode and pi.dev on a local model without thinking about any of that stuff, just running it straight out of the box and it's working great. I have access to an RTX6000 on a remote machine so using it for 'free' and it's replaced Claude for my personal projects. It gets stuck occasionally and gets things wrong from time to time, but so does everything.      reply                             ActorNightly    57 minutes ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  No. All agents out there basically operate on skills, mempry, and tools. There are almost none that do context injection with every prompt. You basically need an external system that modifies every submitted prompt with additional context      reply                             bityard    7 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  > You then need to basically write custom harnesses. Why? Most existing harnesses let you point them at whatever API you want. I'm currently have a great time with Juggler.      reply                             dcrazy    7 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  I find myself having to write a lot more skills for Qwen3.8-27b to effectively use tools it hasn’t seen before, versus virtually no skill-writing needed for Claude anymore. It seems to understand novel tools and when to use them pretty intuitively.      reply                             ActorNightly    7 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  For basic tasks, yea. For more advanced tasks, where the agents running on big llms will do more actions autonomously, the smaller models will sometimes stop execution or go off in wrong directions.      r