ake time to first token awful.      reply                             lnkdinsuxs    1 hour ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  > A RAG solution would make time to first token awful. in your specific workflows, where specifically is the time to first token a dealbreaker? what's your typical target and what's a \"no go\"?      reply                             ActorNightly    8 hours ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Gemma4:31b is better. Qwen is really good at speed (especially with megakernels), but if its going in a wrong direction, it will go in the wrong direction hard. Gemma can self correct with its thinking. The only reason Qwen is more popular is because it can run decently fast on MLX, while Gemma needs a discrete GPU with 24gb of ram to run at usable speeds. Also, theoretically speaking, any small model can be as good as a big model if all they have to do is summarize text. Back before agents were a thing, we had prototype coders running of local inference models from Mistral for ai dev. The workflow was to generate llm summaries  of the codebase, and a summary of changes to be made. Then we would grab search results from the web, feed it to an llm to summarize, and then finally combine all of that context into a final inference run which would generate code, then the codebase would be run automatically, any errors would be llm analyzed, and an llm would generate the next change to be made in the above cycle. Nowdays, we can do that with pi coding agent and Gemma4:31b. But we still have plenty of custom harnesses for things.      reply                             t0mas88    6 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  Realistically a 32GB card is what you need to run Gemma4:31b well. Doing it in 24GB leaves almost no room for context / KV cache.      reply                             ActorNightly    1 hour ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  32k context is plenty  fine. thats 50 pages 