hat existing models are already somewhat decent at it. [0]  https://x.com/o_lalonde/status/2100389409564643518       reply                             orsorna    9 hours ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Why would you want to change weights directly versus running through a new training cycle? For example, I have collected a bunch of data I would like to train weights that I use local inference for. I figured a LoRa would be the way to go, perhaps be more cost effective and generalized too.      reply                             dghlsakjg    8 hours ago       |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  Why would you do a whole new expensive, time-consuming training run if you can just tweak some numbers to get the result you want? I think your use case of incorporating novel data probably calls for training, but I'm sure there are use cases where you can tweak the weights to get what you want (say I wanted to tweak the model to be less sycophantic, or found a way to shrink it by removing weights associated with a language that I don't ever use)      reply                             latentsea    9 hours ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Because text-to-weights is a learnable function. Everything else seems to be. So, why not? Why train weights if you could just prompt for them. If we managed to train image edit models, then why can't we train model-edit models?      reply                             mycall    1 hour ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  The deeper the layers you try to edit, the more compressed it gets, and the harder it is edit.      reply                             MisterMunchkin    9 hours ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  If you could figure out exactly what certain weights do it would be way quicker to directly edit them, whereas training is non-deterministic and takes ages.      reply                             orsorna    9 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  And the modifications of those weights could be tracked in source control and overlayed on top of the base model? I see, there is a lot of potential in that.      reply                             elbear    9 hours ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Because training is more expensive I guess      reply                             parineum    9 hours ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Something someone else brought up in one of the previous threads was to identify the refusal routes and remove them.      reply                             numpad0    9 hours ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  It would take a lot less FLOPS presumably      reply                             smrtinsert    9 hours ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Speed, instant finetunes?  Highly optimized iteration cycles? The applications are the same for any reason why you might want local llms.  This is crazy exciting.      reply                             qwe-askl    8 hours ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Why would it not be able to interpret model weights in such a constructed prompt with tons of information and leading questions? I hope you at least switched off all network access, in case the model cheats and reads HN from yesterday.      reply                             boznz    8 hours ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  All the talk is of the improvement in a capability, but has there been any study on What are these changes are breaking ?      reply                             boznz    8 hours ago       |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  Only asking because I remember injecting bit errors in my local LLM back in 2024 with mixed and sometimes weird results so I definitely knew it was possible; finding out you can do this in a controlled way is frankly mind-blowing.      reply                             tarruda    10 hours ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  I wonder how far this can be taken. Could Qwen 3.8 can interpret and make improvements to Qwen 3.5 0.8B weights, or even its own weights?      reply                             stymaar    9 hours ago       |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  I wouldn't be surprised at all if 3.8-27B could tweak 3.5-0.8B the same way Astra did, but it wouldn't be a big deal either because that part of the paper has nothing to do with directly tweaking the weights like the first part of the paper, but is instead a fairly straightforward abliteration method on a model that is known for a specific pathological behavior (ending up stuck in a thinking loop).      reply                             solnyshok    9 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  isn't consciousness just that, a thinking loop with interruptions for sleep until we break beyond repair? and, just maybe, if we managed to figure out the meaning of life, our thinking would satisfyingly stop?      reply                             f311a    9 hours ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  It can, as long as you can give it benchmarks. \nWhat it can't do is optimize general intelligence without any external validation. You can optimize a model by randomly changing its weights and validating results on your task. A monkey can type a book given enough attempts.      reply                             latentsea    9 hours ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  What I want to see someone try and succeed at would be to get the really highly quantized models to loop less. Many people with only 16GB VRAM are running the IQ3_XXS or IQ3_S quants of Qwen3.8-27B. If it could figure out how to improve those quants, that'd actually be pretty substantial. That's all they did for those improved math results is removed the looping. Why not? Please someone. Please.      reply                             rhdunn    9 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  It's fairly straightforward to write a function that detects