 "page:https://webkit.org/blog/18541/introducing-adaptive-dark-mode/": {
  "v": 3,
  "fetched_at": "2026-11-04T04:19:39.443Z",
  "bytes": 8600,
  "text": " \n \n \n     \n     \n\n     \n     \n\n       Introducing Adaptive Dark Mode | WebKit \n\n     \n\n     \n     \n     \n     \n\n     \n     \n\n     \n     \n     \n     \n\n     \n     \n     \n\n     \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n\n \n \n \n \n\n     \n     \n     \n     \n\n     \n     \n     \n     \n     \n     \n\n     \n     \n     \n     \n     \n     \n     \n     \n     \n     \n  \n \n     \n      \n         \n          WebKit  \n         \n       Downloads  \n    Feature Status  \n \n\t  CSS Features  \n\t  Standards Positions  \n \n \n    Documentation  \n \n\t  Web Inspector  \n\t  Tracking Prevention  \n \n \n    Policies  \n \n\t  Project Goals  \n\t  Bug Prioritization  \n\t  Bug Report Guidelines  \n\t  Code Style Guidelines  \n\t  Commit and Review Policy  \n\t  Feature Policy  \n\t  Security Policy  \n\t  Tracking Prevention Policy  \n\t  WebKit AI Tool Use Policy  \n \n \n    Contribute  \n \n\t  Getting Started  \n\t  Contributing Code  \n\t  Testing Contributions  \n\t  How to Report Bugs  \n\t  GitHub Repository  \n \n \n    Blog  \n \n\t  News Posts  \n\t  CSS Posts  \n\t  Contributing Posts  \n\t  Privacy Posts  \n\t  Performance Posts  \n\t  JavaScript Posts  \n\t  Standards Posts  \n\t  Web Inspector Posts  \n\t  Safari Technology Preview Posts  \n \n \n        \n         \n     \n\n \n     \n    \n         \n              Introducing Adaptive Dark Mode  \n             \n                 Nov 3, 2026 \n                 by  Simon Fraser, Luming Yin, Lily Spiniolas, Joanne Pan, Veda Periwal and Jen Simmons  \n                             \n\n             \n                                \n                 We’re excited to announce Adaptive Dark Mode in Safari 27.3 beta. When enabled by a user, Safari adapts light websites and presents them as dark. \n In the spring of 2019, Safari 12.1 became the first browser to support dark mode on the web. A lot of people love using dark mode, but not every website has a dark version. Almost  40% of web page loads  are from users in dark mode, and yet  only 10%  of page loads support it. If you love dark mode, you’ve probably hit a site that felt particularly  bright . Adaptive Dark Mode is designed to close that gap, providing a consistent dark experience across the web. \n      \n You can try it today in Safari 27.3 beta. To enable, first make sure your system appearance is set to dark. Then browse to a website with a bright background. Safari will offer a preview of Adaptive Dark Mode. Tap ‘Try’. \n For websites that signal support of dark mode with  <meta name=\"color-scheme\" content=\"light dark\">  in HTML or  color-scheme: light dark  in CSS, Adaptive Dark Mode does not change a thing. For the rest of the web, it quickly analyzes the page, looking at colors and estimating whether it’s predominantly light or dark. If the page generally has a dark background with light text, nothing is changed. When a page has a light appearance, Adaptive Dark Mode adapts the page to be dark. \n How does Adaptive Dark Mode work? \n At the core of Adaptive Dark Mode is the transformation of color. Sometimes colors are simply decorative, while other times they have semantic meaning. Adaptive Dark Mode determines when to invert colors so elements remain visible or when to leave colors unchanged. \n While creating Adaptive Dark Mode, we developed a color matrix to invert the luminosity of colors while preserving hue. It’s used to make light backgrounds dark, and dark text light. When transforming text color, Adaptive Dark Mode aims for sufficient contrast as defined by the  WCAG 4.5:1 contrast ratio . Light CSS gradients are also transformed to dark. \n Adaptive Dark Mode makes careful judgments so transformed content looks good and stays usable: buttons have contrast against backgrounds, text is visible over images, and hover menus look clear. \n It takes the same care with images. Most are left unchanged, preserving their integrity. But many icons and logos can lose contrast when the background color behind them is inverted. So Adaptive Dark Mode inspects SVG images and transforms colors when needed. For bitmap images, it analyzes the pixel histograms and inverts simple black-and-white logos. \n    \n  \n Effects like masks and  mix-blend-mode  can create undesirable results on an element whose colors have been transformed, so in some cases Adaptive Dark Mode turns such effects off. Also,  <canvas>  content is left unchanged, since canvases contain a wide range of content that’s hard to predict. \n It’s precisely because Adaptive Dark Mode is happening deep within the WebKit browser engine that we are able to find creative ways to solve the most complex challenges, apply the transformations with great speed and power efficiency, all while rigorously protecting user privacy. \n Making your site work even better \n In general, a web page will work well with Adaptive Dark Mode if it follows good modern web development practices. \n If your site still uses older techniques, small updates can help. For logos and icons, swap bitmap images for SVG. Use solid or gradient colors in CSS instead of tiled background images. Replace image sprites with modern CSS for rounded corners, shadows, and button states. Create content images with transparent backgrounds instead of baking the page background color into the image. Add padding to containers to prevent crowding. \n Perhaps you want a specific dark mode experience for a certain portion of the page. Apply   color-scheme: light dark   to the relevant element and add your own dark styles. \n Building your own dark mode \n For complete control over how your site looks in dark mode, add full support. It’s a terrific time to do so. It’s never been easier. \n      Here’s an example of a website in light mode (left) and with Adaptive Dark Mode applied (center). If the site creates a custom dark mode, it could look like the third version (right).  \n All the web technology needed to fully support dark mode is widely available today, including: \n \n   <meta name=\"color-scheme\" content=\"light dark\">   \n   :root { color-scheme: light dark }    \n   @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) { }    \n   <picture><source media=\"(prefers-color-scheme: dark)\">   \n   light-dark()    \n \n Newer CSS functions like   color-mix()   and   contrast-color()  , plus popular techniques for organizing design systems with CSS variables provide additional options and flexibility. \n Be sure to declare dark mode support using the  color-scheme  meta tag in your HTML head and/or by setting the  color-scheme  property on  :root  or  body  in your CSS. Don’t wait to inject this information later via JavaScript. This best practice helps ensure your site is rendered correctly as fast as possible. \n Using the “light only” color scheme \n If you would like your web app or website to not be transformed by Adaptive Dark Mode, you can communicate this using  color-scheme . The HTML  <meta name=\"color-scheme\" content=\"light only\">  tag lets browsers know that 1) this page supports  light  mode and 2) should  only  be displayed in light mode. The  color-scheme: light only  rule does the same thing, but with the flexibility of CSS, allowing you to just target specific elements on the page — or of course, you can target the whole page with  :root { } . (Note: the ‘only’ keyword is not supported in Safari 27.3 beta 1. Check back as Adaptive Dark Mode evolves through the beta process.) \n Protecting user privacy \n Apple firmly believes user privacy is a fundamental human right. If websites could detect when a user has enabled Adaptive Dark Mode, that would increase the amount of fingerprintable information and weaken privacy protections against trackers. \n As a result, Safari prevents websites from detecting when Adaptive Dark Mode has been applied. JavaScript that queries CSS colors will get the original authored colors, not the transformed ones. Any images drawn into canvas render in their original form. \n Feedback \n If you manage a website or web app, and would like to discuss how Adaptive Dark Mode is adapting your project, feel free to reach out at  web-evangelist@apple.com . \n If you try out Adaptive Dark Mode as a user, and find a site that does not work well, we’d love for you to file a report with  Feedback Assistant . (Learn more about  filing Feedback ). \n\n                             \n         \n\n         \n             \n                                     \n         \n         \n             \n               Previously  WebKit Features for Safari 27.2  Learn more                \n         \n\n    \n    \n      \n  \n\n \n     \n             @webkit@front-end.social  \n  Site Map  \n  Privacy Policy  \n  Licensing WebKit  \n  WebKit and the WebKit logo are trademarks of Apple Inc.  \n   \n     \n \n\n\n \n \n \n "
 },
 "page:https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50358562": {
  "v": 3,
  "fetched_at": "2026-11-04T04:19:42.108Z",
  "bytes": 64978,
  "text": "        Safari introduces Adaptive Dark Mode in 27.3 | Hacker News                  Hacker News   new  |  past  |  comments  |  ask  |  show  |  jobs  |  submit      login                           Safari introduces Adaptive Dark Mode in 27.3   (  webkit.org  )          343 points  by  paddingtone    6 hours ago      |  hide  |  past  |  favorite  |  196 comments                          help    \n      \n                    kickingvegas    6 hours ago       |  next   [–]       \n  FYI, light-dark() in CSS is a thing now.  https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Reference/V...       reply                             kps    6 hours ago       |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  And `:prefers-color-scheme` has been supported by all major browsers for seven years.      reply                             cosmotic    5 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  Windows 3 through windows 7 had pervasive support for dark mode as long as application developers didn't custom paint their user interfaces. Application developers started out  almost all  supporting system colors and gradually over the next 25 years or so started implementing their own custom-drawn interfaces, accelerated by embedded IE/mshtml views and then later even more by Electron. This concluded with practically zero interfaces supporting the system color scheme.      reply                             da_chicken    5 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  Yeah but \"dark mode\" for Win3 through WinXP was primarily to support passive matrix LCDs, which required  inverted  color schemes to appear with enough contrast to read. That is why the built-in \"high contrast\" color schemes look so aggressively awful.      reply                             toyg    5 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Yeah, when dark mode became \"a thing\" my first reaction was \"...didn't we have this already?\" I was a theme junkie back in Windows 98 times, but stopped bothering since it became increasingly clear that Microsoft was not really interested in supporting them after XP.      reply                             Rohansi    4 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Custom painting isn't the only thing that broke it. You can customize the style (colors) of the native widgets to get a desired look but IIRC you could choose from system colors (follows the theme/mode automatically) or exact colors. Some applications used a mix of them which made the UI look awful if you switched away from whatever the default theme was.      reply                             jansan    6 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  And broken in Chrome since the beginning:  https://issues.chromium.org/issues/366412383       reply                             zamadatix    5 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  I wouldn't call this part of the situation a bug. I'm sure everyone has their own opinion about which is the \"right\" way to handle a mismatch of OS level preference and browser level theme though. The underlying problem is Chrome exposes no (normal) setting to override the OS level setting at all, not necessarily that the preference shall always follow the browser's theme specifically.      reply                             omer_balyali    5 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  This issue is related to appearance setting inside Chrome itself (which sets Chrome's appearance, not how it should work with websites AFAIK). The core feature of prefers-color-scheme (following the OS-level setting) is working as intended.      reply                             jansan    5 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  It is not true that prefers-color-scheme should always follow the OS-level setting. The doc says \"The method by which the user expresses their preference can vary. It might be a system-wide setting exposed by the Operating System, or a setting controlled by the user agent.\"[1] So if the user sets dark mode in the browser, do they not explicitly express their preference for websites? How could they be more explicit about the setting? [1] https://drafts.csswg.org/mediaqueries-5/#prefers-color-schem...       reply                             omer_balyali    4 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  I didn't meant that \"only OS-level setting should be respected\", what I meant is that Chrome's setting is how Chrome themes should look like, not for websites. Chrome already has a setting to change a specific website's color-scheme, although it's only working when Developer Tools is visible (then with Cmd+Shift+P \"Toggle CSS media feature prefers-color-scheme\"), so CSS implementation actually follows respecting UA-level preference over OS-level setting. But again, Chrome doesn't have a feature that lets users to choose which color-scheme they want to browse the web (so there is no bug, but it's a UX issue because their Appearance settings doesn't make it clear to the users what this setting changes) other than the setting they gave to developers. Honestly I would love to see all browsers have an option for how color-scheme settings should apply to websites.      reply                             omer_balyali    3 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  Yes it's confusing for the end-user, as I said they should improve their UX in that setting page to make it clear what this setting changes. However, your original comment was saying that prefers-color-scheme is bugged in Chrome is not true. It works as intended: it follows both UA-level and OS-level settings correctly. The settings in Appearance tab is not a setting for preference when browsing the web. Many apps have a setting to choose if their app should change appearance when OS setting changed (or to override it), they could simply leave out this setting and let Chrome chrome (app ui) always follow the OS setting or always be in default light mode, instead they gave users to choose \"how their Chrome browser will look like\", not \"how every website they visit should look like\".      reply                             jansan    4 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  You can set the appearance mode (light, dark, device) under chrome://settings/appearance . If you change the mode, the settings page (which is a website) adopts that mode, so a user would naturally assume this is also the mode for displaying web content.      reply                             kulahan    5 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  So I guess it hasn’t been supported anywhere in a sense. Safari and Firefox are pretty small.      reply                             akersten    5 hours ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  It makes me so angry that they named the CSS function `light-dark` instead of leaving the door open to allowing generic user- or site-specified color schemes. I mean it was  right there  in all the other mechanisms around this feature, they just had to read and think about why these are structured the way they are, and why \"light\" and \"dark\" were always  values  and not  keywords  in all of these..:       - <meta name=\"color-scheme\" content=\"light dark\">\n    \n    - :root { color-scheme: light dark }\n\n    - @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) { }\n\n    - <picture><source media=\"(prefers-color-scheme: dark)\">\n  \n.... and then we've got `light-dark()`, the goofy looking dragon hanging out to the side of these majestic, forward-thinking parts of the specification. Did they not look at the prior art and notice that \"one of these things is not like the other?\" All the other pieces of this puzzle, conceivably, could have one day had something like `:root { color-scheme: halloween }` or `@media (prefers-color-scheme: supercontrast)` but no, we're just going to cement `light-dark()` as the only two possible color schemes we'd ever want to switch between forever.      reply                             omer_balyali    2 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  Color schemes (luminance modes) and color themes (aesthetic styles) are not the same thing. Halloween theme can have light and dark schemes. Also, prefers-contrast media query exists, which can be used in combination with the luminance modes (color-scheme): halloween-light, halloween-dark, halloween-light-high-contrast, halloween-dark-high-contrast.  https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=47832705       reply                             tshaddox    3 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  I’m not sure what kind of function you’re asking for that would support arbitrary user-defined themes. With the if() function and/or @function declarations you could build pretty much any system you want for defining theme-specific values for arbitrary themes.      reply                             have_faith    5 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  It’s always irked me that light and dark are given privileged positions in the namespace. They’re just themes (or schemes if you like), like all the other possible themes.      reply                             hexasquid    3 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Web standards are discussed in the open aren't they?\nI'll see if I can find what reasoning lead to this.      reply                             hexasquid    2 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n   https://github.com/w3c/csswg-drafts/issues/7561  appears to be a place to start digging      reply                             nsoam    6 hours ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  I want the opposite. I suffer from astigmatism, which makes white on black hard to read. I wish Safari could turn dark mode websites into light mode websites.      reply                             jimbobimbo    5 hours ago       |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  I have similar issue with dark mode applied to content - hurts my eyes. I prefer to have window chrome in dark, to make \"fade away\". The only application which allows me to have my cake and eat it is Firefox: I can run Windows in dark yet read web in light mode. I wish Windows would steal this feature for the whole OS: I don't want Notepad to use dark for text, but per-app setting usually turns the whole app light.      reply                             0x1F8B    4 hours ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  I have 20/20 but for some reason cannot handle dark mode except for two things, terminal and coding and I cannot do light mode for either of those. But whether it is my os ui or my phone ui, I feel like I can't distinguish borders and boundaries in the UI as well as I can with light mode.      reply                             cj00    3 hours ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Yeah I can’t do dark mode websites because they leave an after image for some time after reading. So I made a Force Light Mode extension that just applies an inverse to any site that forces dark mode on me. Safari 27 now has some Siri extension builder, maybe it can build it for you.      reply                             marcellosso    6 hours ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  I use dark mode for everything, so I'm pretty happy about this. Some sites were clearly designed to be light, and forcing dark on them could break the experience, but that's exactly what `color-scheme: light only` is for, and it's good that Safari respects it. Other than that it feels like a win-win to me: great for dark mode users, and for devs not much changes, you just add \"light only\" if you don't want your site to be affected.      reply                             greazy    5 hours ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Everyone should checkout Dark Reader extension, it's been doing adaptive dark mode for many years, and it's customisable. It even does light mode too. Using it in Firefox on HN right now!      reply                             ckdot    5 hours ago       |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  Slows down loading of websites a lot though.      reply                             Telaneo    5 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  A slowly loading website is preferable to a quick flashbang.      reply                             chrisra    5 hours ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  I'm concerned that these extensions can \"read and modify\" everything. I can't use that for work.      reply                             lnvdpwl    5 hours ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  I just noticed that Dark Reader stopped working for me. Turns out I accidentally installed \"Dark Mode\", a potentially malicious clone that got blocked :/      reply                             tehnub    5 hours ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  I like that on Chrome, and while it works on Safari, I prefer Noir there.      reply                             accelbred    4 hours ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  AFAIK, its detectable by the web page though      reply                             NooneAtAll5    4 hours ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Dark Reader went political - that's an automatic \"NO\" from me      reply                             kstrauser    2 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  What do you mean?      reply                             voxic11    28 minutes ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  They posted about the was in Ukraine being bad and their disappointment with the Belarusian governments participation in it.  https://darkreader.org/blog/no-war/       reply                             macNchz    5 hours ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Personally I'm excited for this on iOS—I find that reading boring stuff in dark mode at minimum brightness on my phone is  easily  the most effective way for me to fall asleep (contrary to the blue light/no phones in bed advice). With an OLED screen the amount of light emitted is so much lower than any alternative I've tried. For sites like HN (some threads on HN are pretty boring) that don't have a native dark mode, my solution has been setting up the \"Accessibility Shortcut\" feature to invert the colors on the screen. Three clicks on the lock button and the page inverts. It works  okay , but it requires manual intervention for each site depending on whether it supports dark mode.      reply                             mh-    4 hours ago       |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  I have been using a Safari extension called Noir on iPhone for years. Works great on HN and most sites. I have it set to a pure black for HN. Easy to disable per-site when it fails. It syncs to my phone's dark mode. I also read in bed as you've described, with Night Shift turned on and very low brightness. It's great.      reply                             Reason077    5 hours ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Hacker News itself is a prime example of a website that doesn’t support dark mode, but could do so trivially (and, IMO, would look so much better if it did!)      reply                             quantumink    5 hours ago       |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  My HN is in dark mode, though the honors for that accomplishment go to the duckduck browser. Been reading HN on dark mode a while now (light mode burns me eyes, only way to use HN for me now is with the Duck) P.S. the Duck browser literally has a universal dark mode setting, I don't recall a site it wasn't able to dark-force darkify for me (language is fun)      reply                             ivanjermakov    5 hours ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  There is no downside to having prefers-color-scheme media query set up, even for HN. Too bad still roughly 1 in 4 popular websites support it.      reply                             NooneAtAll5    4 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  no, choosing light/dark mode should be manual      reply                             Rohansi    4 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  Per website...? Why would anyone want that instead of just using the system-wide preference? Many sites/apps have an option of system/light/dark.      reply                             hackinthebochs    2 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  For me, most dark mode styles are awful (jet black background bright white text). I'd rather have per website toggle so I can apply my own styling when the site's styles suck.      reply                             codazoda    4 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Hard disagree here. Follow my system default.      reply                             entropie    3 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  Iam a both kind of guy.      reply                             eek2121    3 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  No, it should follow system defaults with an option for a manual override. People like you are the reason the internet is broken.      reply                             internet2000    18 minutes ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  This feature works well on Hacker News, just trying it on the beta.      reply                             matthova    2 hours ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  The only chrome extension I have is to make hackernews support dark mode. Love having it I do appreciate how minimalist hackernews tries to be, but I think respecting the operating system's theme can be considered very minimalist      reply                             therealmarv    5 hours ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]   