ach theme. But I'm really fine with letting the computer figure it out. CRM, back office tools, admin dashboards... many clients are approving one design and dark mode is an afterthought. Why can't the computer just... do it for me, and do a better than passable job at that.      reply                             hinkley    3 hours ago       |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  Modern CSS has some simpler mechanisms to deal with the light/dark dichotomy now. I translated a blog to support dark mode recently and it wasn't that difficult. The two sticking points were <code> and picking foreground colors that didn't blend in, and the github icon. The other was Github's icon. But `filter: invert(1)` does a pretty decent job of handling that as long as the icons are black and white.      reply                             drusepth    5 hours ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Really happy to see this. Users begged me to add dark mode to my sites for years and it's been nothing but a pain to support since adding it. If other browsers pick up something similar, I look forward to the day I can finally remove \"manual\" dark mode support and just leave it up to the browser.      reply                             pamcake    5 hours ago       |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  > I look forward to the day I can finally remove \"manual\" dark mode support and just leave it up to the browser. Today it is!  https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Reference/V...   https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Reference/A...       reply                             saagarjha    5 hours ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  I guess if you truly don’t care about it sure, this is probably going to be an improvement, but if you are willing to invest even a bit of effort I strongly think you’d want to decide how your website looks like in dark mode.      reply                             drusepth    5 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  I'd estimate I've probably spent 100+ hours testing and fixing dark mode issues over the past 5-10 years. Investing \"a little bit of effort\" is how I got myself into the trap of doubling+ UI QA time to make sure everything _also_ looks good in dark mode (and also in dark mode across a matrix of other configurations) every time I want to ship anything new to users. I know some people care about dark mode (and that's why I bit the bullet to add it), but I'd honestly much rather have spent all that time working on actual features.      reply                             reader9274    6 hours ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Finally a fix for HN      reply                             kkotak    6 hours ago       |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  Please! I don't use Safari so this won't help me, but switching back to HN from other mostly dark themed sites hurts my eyes :(      reply                             tiffanyh    2 hours ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  What’s the relationship for Safari and Safari Tech Preview. Because correct me if I’m wrong, neither of these features were in the Tech Preview. And numerous features have been in Tech Preview that have never made it into mainline Safari.      reply                             stwrt    2 hours ago       |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  Safari Technology Preview is primarily for testing the latest WebKit features, rather than previewing every upcoming Safari feature. Safari Beta is generally what you'd want to use to test features coming to the next Safari release. And yes, features appearing in Technology Preview aren't necessarily guaranteed to ship in Safari.      reply                             famfamfam    5 hours ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Chrome also has a functional adaptive dark mode (no extension required!), but it is hidden behind an experimental flag `enable-force-dark` as an origin trial on desktop. I am surprised that the Chrome team have not surfaced this to users earlier (outside of Android Chrome, where it is available as a 'Dark Mode' toggle). To try it, go to chrome://flags and search for Dark Mode, then enable Auto Dark Mode for Web Contents. Alternatively, you can toggle it on using the Chrome DevTools Rendering tab, as \"Enable automatic dark mode\"      reply                             arccy    5 hours ago       |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  Some pages break badly... like google docs and some QR codes      reply                             pazimzadeh    5 hours ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Finally.. In dark mode, any windows that aren't dark can be huge eyesores. I recently made a menu bar utility to enable per-window color invert for this reason:  https://github.com/pazimzadeh/WindowInvert/  It helps for apps that don't have dark mode yet (Pages, etc) and even some apps that do (Preview has a dark mode, but it doesn't work on old PDF's).      reply                             etatester    53 minutes ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Really exciting! Now do apps.      reply                             senderista    5 hours ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  I just wish all websites supported reader mode.      reply                             bla3    6 hours ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  I know it's always been this way, but it's weird to me that Apple uses the WebKit blog for Safari product announcements.      reply                             kccqzy    5 hours ago       |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  It’s not. The feature is implemented deep within WebKit so it makes sense to be on this blog. > It’s precisely because Adaptive Dark Mode is happening deep within the WebKit browser engine that we are able to find creative ways to solve the most complex challenges, apply the transformations with great speed and power efficiency      reply                             gooseyman    3 hours ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  This is great. I use the brave beta appearance option \"Dark Mode\" and every now and then a site is almost unreadable.      reply                             kccqzy    5 hours ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  A pet peeve of mine is websites thinking oh you prefer dark mode so let’s make the background pure black. I cannot take it because the contrast is too much: #fff on #000 is way more uncomfortable than #000 on #fff for me. There are certain developers who continue to think that dark mode is for conserving battery life with an OLED screen so pure black is preferable. I hate that so much that I have turned off dark mode globally. If websites can’t be bothered to implement an actually nice dark theme like Solarized Dark, I’d rather prefer the light mode. Besides Solarized Dark, GitHub’s Soft Dark theme is quite well done, but unfortunately it’s not the default for a logged-out user with dark mode. So with dark mode I need to log in to GitHub to get acceptable colors. Now that I think about it, I need a feature to limit the darkness of colors anywhere on the page, background or text except images. Remap #000 to #222 and #fff to #ddd.      reply                             ayaros    2 hours ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Oh man! Dark reader's getting Sherlocked now!      reply                             accelbred    4 hours ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  This looks awesome! I especially like that its not detectectable by the page for privacy! I want this in firefox.      reply                             dawnerd    4 hours ago       |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  They say it can’t be detected but give it time and someone will figure out some signal that gives it away.      reply                             mrbonner    4 hours ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  I wonder when Apple will separate Safari update from iOS update. They have done this for the MacOS.      reply                             nixass    4 hours ago       |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  I wonder when they will separate other browsers on iOS from clusterfxxk Safari is      reply                             Transcendium    6 hours ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  I fear this will lead to a lot of broken websites. That seems to be the case with a lot of the browser plugins that provide the same functionality.      reply                             kevincox    6 hours ago       |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  1. It's opt-in. The user is choosing the occasional broken site over light themes. 2. The site can always provide their own dark theme if the automatic one is broken.      reply                             arcanemachiner    6 hours ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  There's a mode like this in an Android Chrome fork called Cromite that works surprisingly well. Most of the other options do suck though.      reply                             vinnymac    6 hours ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  If this ends up working anything like the Gmail Dark Mode implementations on iOS and Android that have drove developers insane and users mad, then I expect both parties to become upset. It is easy to view this as a win for users, but the moment this doesn't work well in various scenarios, the vocal minority will punish the developers of the web as if they did something wrong here, and everyone loses. Here is to hoping that is not the case, and they did a good job with the implementation in Safari, but I have my suspicions.      reply                             aaomidi    6 hours ago       |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  >will punish the developers of the web as if they did something wrong here Yes, developers can solve this by simply implementing dark mode.      reply                             vinnymac    6 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  Your imagination for what can go wrong seems limited in my eyes. I've been in the trenches where a specification was not followed, or was wrong from the beginning. That can be the case here.      reply                             01100011    3 hours ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Can we get apple to enforce consistent 'back' behavior in iOS next? I just switched after being an Android user since the dawn of the smartphone era. There are some improvements over Android so I'll give them credit but holy hell is navigation just a complete mess. Every app seems to behave differently, and even within the same app you'll see inconsistencies. Would it really be that hard to provide a universal back gesture that works across apps like Android has? It makes the phone so clunkly and slow.      reply                             grounder    6 hours ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  I've always wanted to use Safari as my default browser. I just need them to fix the disappearing favicons in the sidebar tabs. Apple: please fix.      reply                             guessmyname    6 hours ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  • As a user: great • As a web developer: what a nightmare, now your website will look different to different people, as if having to support multiple browser engines and multiple screen rations, and multiple platforms, etc. was not a big pile of problems already. • As someone who doesn’t like dark-mode due to eye strain: good-good… for people who like it ¯\\_(ツ)_/¯      reply                             isityettime    6 hours ago       |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  > what a nightmare, now your website will look different to different people That's what the web has always been. People don't all have the same display types, the same subpixel layouts, the same display sizes, the same DPIs, the same fonts, the same zoom levels... People use reader modes, global restyling extensions, per-site custom style sheets, full screen magnifiers, bounded/partial magnifiers, screen readers, OS high-contrast modes... the web has never been a fixed canvas that you get to paint on and then hand to users.      reply                             mortenjorck    6 hours ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  I like to think of it as a gentle encouragement to finally implement prefers-color-scheme in web applications. (I say this as a designer who has spent countless hours on dark-mode palettes as well as someone who has the automatic circadian toggle enabled everywhere.)      reply                             apsurd    6 hours ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  disagree. the web specifically is built as a dynamic medium for consumption. The fluid nature is the point. Yes, it is harder for developers to force a consistent experience, but as a professional web dev, we should not treat the web as a fixed medium.      reply                             cluckindan    6 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  As long as a client needs to approve a layout, that is not happening      reply                             Telaneo    6 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  Sounds like a problem with the client.      reply                             cluckindan    6 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  A web agency is not going to start questioning the brand book of a 100x larger client.      reply                             Telaneo    5 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  I'm not going to publically question my boss doing stupid shit, but that doesn't make him not stupid.      reply                             vulcan01    6 hours ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Re: web developer > The HTML <meta name=\"color-scheme\" content=\"light only\"> tag lets browsers know that 1) this page supports light mode and 2) should only be displayed in light mode.      reply                             kps    6 hours ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  As a web developer, all you have to do is support `:prefers-color-scheme`.      reply                             Spivak    6 hours ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Even as a developer I'm all for it, go even harder. We may eventually reach the promised land where the user agent defines so much of the final appearance things like \"pixel perfect design\" or beating standard controls into within an inch of their lives to fit some design system are a thing of the past.      reply                             threetonesun    6 hours ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Wait until you learn I can change the font size and even override the site CSS on my own machine!      reply                             guessmyname    3 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  Who cares if you can? I am referring to non-tech-savvy web users who barely know what YouTube is: your parents, grandparents, accountant, or city firefighters. There are literally millions of people in this planet who don’t even know what HTML is. The commenters above appear to be criticizing my remark as if I was mistaken. Software developers often lack imagination when it comes to real-world problems.      reply                             loloquwowndueo    6 hours ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Does this also allow me to lighten sites that default to extremely hard to read dark mode? I wish the crusade to make everything dark would end. So do others.  https://xkcd.com/3227/       reply                             _boffin_    6 hours ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  > Safari prevents websites from detecting when Adaptive Dark Mode has been applied. JavaScript that queries CSS colors will get the original authored colors, not the transformed ones. Any images drawn into canvas render in their original form. So… the browser is now intentionally lying now. Ain’t that great.      reply                             mirashii    6 hours ago       |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  Browsers already intentionally lie all the time, and have had to for years, because fingerprinting and tracking by various ad networks and bad actors has been rampant.      reply                             mortenjorck    6 hours ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  This is more or less unavoidable to avoid breaking poorly-architected front-ends that rely on styling to handle logic.      reply                             kccqzy    6 hours ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Browsers lie all the time. Browsers also prevent sites from getting the color of a link because visited links can be shown in a different color and this can be used by the site to tell if a user has ever visited a link. Don’t you love it when browsers lie to websites to enhance the user’s privacy.      reply                             morsch    6 hours ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  It's the user's agent      reply                             neilalexander    6 hours ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  One less thing to fingerprint.      reply                             kvuj    6 hours ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  > So… the browser is now intentionally lying now. Ain’t that great. To the server. I'm 100% behind this. The unholy amount of control websites have over our web browser should go down, not up.      reply                             giancarlostoro    6 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  > To the server. I'm 100% behind this. The unholy amount of control websites have over our web browser should go down, not up. Especially when so many lack reasonable ADA compliance.      reply                             hendersoon    5 hours ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Hah, I just paid for an addon to do this, Noir. Sure you could do it for free but it's a nice experience. Oh well, no harm done.      reply                             charcircuit    5 hours ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  This is much better than the absolute embarrassment Mozilla tried with alternate page styles. I swear no one at Mozilla ever tested out the feature considering the style you select does not get persisted so the next time you navigate it reverts back to the original style. Meanwhile this features is actually tired to a persisted device setting that doesn't get reset every time you go to a new page.      reply                             cyanydeez    6 hours ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Do they plan to introduce compliance with PWAs any time this decade?      reply                             inshard    6 hours ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Thank you, whoever led this!      reply                             cluckindan    6 hours ago       |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  On the contrary, screw you whoever led it. Who even wants authored styles nowadays.      reply                             arcanemachiner    6 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  \"Authored\"? It just applies a set of rules to the existing stylesheet. It's not like they're customizing it for different websites.      reply                             cluckindan    4 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  It was a rhetorical question, hence no question mark.      reply                             roflchoppa    6 hours ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  take the wikipedia, no more flashbangs for me HA!      reply                             sheept    6 hours ago       |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  Wikipedia already supports dark mode as a feature of the default Vector 2022 skin.[0] [0]:  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Dark_mode       reply                             roflchoppa    5 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  I guess this time suffering was really a choice :(      reply                             kccqzy    5 hours ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Wikipedia allows you to write any custom CSS. In 2012/2013 when Facebook IPO’ed and was the cool thing I made a user CSS that made Wikipedia look like Facebook.      reply                             nouman0x45    6 hours ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  damn      reply                             russellbeattie    6 hours ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  \"Websites aren't making dark mode versions! WE'LL DO IT FOR THEM!\" This has to be the result of some passion project by yet another whacko dark mode fanatic (you know who you are). Non-standard, assuredly buggy, and basically a maintenance nightmare. Good luck with that craziness. Can we have another setting which turns dark websites into actually readable light designs for adults?      reply                             I_am_tiberius    6 hours ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Is this a headline form the year 2015?      reply                             prh8    5 hours ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  For anyone using Zen browser, you can accomplish this on a site by site basis using the newer \"Boosts\" feature available via the extensions button in the url bar Personally, I find it close to perfect for Gmail and HN, among others      reply                             drfloyd51    5 hours ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  It is truly arrogant of Apple to change a websites presentation, graphics, logos, and then tell website authors if you want to opt out just do XYZ. But this Apple arrogance is also why they are Apple. And why you are using a software keyboard and a phone shaped like a dark mirror.      reply                             accelbred    4 hours ago       |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  Its truly arrogant of website authors to think they should have the final say on how users view the site on their browser.      reply                             joshuat    6 hours ago       |  prev   [–]       \n  This is surely going to be optional, so it won't impact me in the slightest, but it is a pet peeve of mine when browsers (or browser extensions, looking at you 1Password) alter the UI of websites away from how they were originally designed without explicit instruction to do so. This sounds like it meets that threshold, it will just surely be a hit-or-miss mess. Hopefully if it makes it to iOS it will drive more site owners to adapt to `prefers-color-scheme`.      reply                             JoshTriplett    6 hours ago       |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  Browsers are user-agents, not website-agents. By way of example, if a website shows ads or does tracking, I will  absolutely  alter it to my liking.      reply                             wpm    5 hours ago       |  parent  |  prev   [–]       \n  I and my browser are allowed to do whatever I want with the data that is posted on the public internet.      reply                             joshuat    3 hours ago       |  root  |  parent   [–]       \n  I'm very in favor of users being able to do whatever they want with websites - I'm all for it. I just don't like extensions making changes without a clean way to disable it, like in 1P      reply             \n            \n   Guidelines  |  FAQ  |  Lists  |  API  |  Security  |  Legal  |  Apply to YC  |  Contact    \n Search:           "
