  "text": " \n \n\n     [2611.04507] Signals: Continuous Diagnostics of AI Agents \n   \n   \n   \n   \n   \n   \n   \n   \n   \n   \n   \n \n    \n    \n    \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n          \n   \n   \n   \n               \n \n\n  \n     Skip to main content \n  \n \n \n \n    \n   \n     \n   \n   \n     \n     Search \n   \n   \n     \n   \n   \n     Submit \n     Donate \n      \n Log in    \n \n \n   \n     \n       Search arXiv \n       \n       \n       \n     \n     \n      Press Enter to search &middot;  Advanced search \n     \n   \n \n\n     \n       \n \n     \n     \n--> \n\n   \n     \n       Computer Science > Artificial Intelligence \n     \n\n     \n       arXiv:2611.04507  (cs)\n     \n \n \n   \n     \n  [Submitted on 3 Nov 2026] \n      Title: Signals: Continuous Diagnostics of AI Agents \n      Authors:  Amey Rambatla  (1),  Vatsal Goel  (1),  Sagar Gaur  (1),  Siddhant Loya  (1),  Anurag Mishra  (1),  Asif Mohammad  (1) ((1) Cisco)              View a PDF of the paper titled Signals: Continuous Diagnostics of AI Agents, by Amey Rambatla (1) and 5 other authors \n     View PDF \n     HTML (experimental) \n\n\n\n     \n             Abstract: AI agents can make incorrect decisions or waste resources even when their tools and infrastructure work normally. Their nondeterministic behavior makes these problems difficult to reproduce and diagnose. We developed Signals to identify such problems from agentic traces and suggest concrete remediations. We built the method around a taxonomy of 45 problem types across eight families, with definitions and evidence requirements for each type. The method distinguishes realized failures, risk exposures, and inefficiencies, groups related findings into engineering issues called Signals, and assesses their severity from the supporting evidence. Each Signal includes actions a developer can take and checks for evaluating the change. Further executions can add evidence to an existing Signal or reveal a new problem. We evaluated the method on public agent executions and achieved 91.43% F1 when matching Signals to reference issues, about 18 percentage points higher than a one-pass LLM control that used the same model and taxonomy.\n     \n\n     \n     \n                \n           Comments: \n           11 pages, 1 figure, 6 tables \n         \n \n           Subjects: \n           \n             Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)  \n          \n           Cite as: \n             arXiv:2611.04507  [cs.AI]  \n         \n         \n             \n           (or  \n               arXiv:2611.04507v1  [cs.AI]  for this version)\n           \n         \n         \n             \n                          https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2611.04507  \n               \n                 \n                 Focus to learn more \n               \n               \n               \n                                    arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite (pending registration) \n             \n           \n          \n     \n   \n \n     \n       Submission history  From: Amey Rambatla [ view email ]            [v1] \n        Tue, 3 Nov 2026 08:32:43 UTC (51 KB) \n \n   \n   \n      \n        \n       Full-text links: \n       Access Paper: \n       \n   \nView a PDF of the paper titled Signals: Continuous Diagnostics of AI Agents, by Amey Rambatla (1) and 5 other authors   View PDF    HTML (experimental)    TeX Source\n    \n        \n           \n           view license \n          \n     \n          \n       Additional Features \n       \n          Audio Summary  \n       \n     \n     \n     Current browse context: \n   cs.AI \n\n   \n \n       < prev \n     \n       |        \n       next > \n      \n    \n     new \n      |  \n     recent \n      |   2026-11 \n    \n    Change to browse by:\n     \n         cs  \n     \n   \n\n     \n       \n         References & Citations \n         \n            SciX    Google Scholar  \n            Semantic Scholar  \n         \n          